  "narrative": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\"><em>The Irish Against the War</em> is a 2024 scholarly monograph published by Peter Lang Verlag as part of the established <em>Reimagining Ireland</em> series. The work applies postcolonial theory and social movement analysis to trace Irish anti-war activism from the 1950s through contemporary conflicts. As a recent publication, it has not yet accumulated measurable citation activity, though it is discoverable through library catalogues and integrated into the academic publishing ecosystem.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Marie-Violaine Louvet's monograph addresses a gap in scholarship by examining how Irish civil society has mobilized against international conflicts over seven decades. The study begins with the formation of the Irish Campaign for Nuclear Disarmament in 1958 and extends to contemporary responses to wars including the current conflict in Ukraine. By synthesizing postcolonial critical perspectives with social movement theory, the work positions Ireland's anti-war activism as a distinct geopolitical and cultural phenomenon, shaped by the nation's own historical experience of colonialism and independence.</p>\n\n<p class=\"story-text\">Published within the <em>Reimagining Ireland</em> series\u2014a peer-reviewed sequence that includes works on Irish fiction, history, and cultural conflict\u2014the book benefits from the series' established editorial standards and scholarly audience. Companion volumes in the series address related themes of Irish identity, literature, and social movements, suggesting a cohesive intellectual community interested in this work's subject matter and analytical approach.</p>\n\n<p class=\"story-text\">Library holdings data indicate the book is held by at least three institutions internationally, confirming its entry into academic collections. The work is registered in OpenAIRE and appears in library discovery systems, making it accessible to researchers and students. Citation tracking indicates no recorded scholarly references yet, which is consistent with the book's recent publication date and reflects the lag time typical for monograph uptake in academic literature.</p>\n\n<p class=\"story-text\">The scholarly foundations of this work\u2014its primary research base, theoretical framework integrating postcolonial and movement studies, and coverage of an underexamined aspect of modern Irish political history\u2014position it as a reference for historians, Irish studies scholars, and researchers examining peace activism and international relations. Early visibility within the specialized <em>Reimagining Ireland</em> community and potential adoption in Irish history and political science courses will likely shape its trajectory in coming years.</p>",
